hadiths[6]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3057","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Isa, who narrated to us Salim, who said: Then Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, on the authority of Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm al-Ansari, on the authority of Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, on the authority of his uncle Nafi' ibn Jubayr, on the authority of his father Jubayr ibn Mut'im (RA), who said: ""I indeed saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) before revelation was sent down to him, and he was standing on his camel at Arafat with the people, departing from there along with them. This was nothing but a divinely guided bestowal of grace from Allah."" Abu Bakr (RA) said: ""His statement 'before revelation was sent down to him' most likely refers to either before the verse 'Then depart from where the people depart' (Surah Al-Baqarah 2:199) was revealed to him, or before the entire Quran was revealed to him, and this is the evidence for the correctness of this interpretation."""
"3058","Narrated to us Salamah ibn Junādah who said: Narrated to us Abū Muʿāwiyah, from Hishām, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:
""Quraysh and those who followed their religion would stand at Muzdalifah, and they were called al-Ḥums. The rest of the Arabs would stand at ʿArafah. When Islam came, Allah commanded His Prophet (ﷺ) to come to ʿArafah and stand there, then depart from it.""

She said: ""This is the meaning of the verse:
*‘Then depart from where the people depart.’* (Qur’an 2:199)

This report indicates that Allah, the Exalted, commanded His Prophet (ﷺ) specifically to stand at ʿArafah and to oppose the Quraysh in their standing at Muzdalifah, abandoning their exit from the Ḥaram (Sacred Precinct) because they named themselves al-Ḥums. This verse—*‘Then depart from where the people depart’*—refers to those other than Quraysh who used to stand at Muzdalifah. This usage is of the type where the word ‘people’ may refer to some of them, since it is known that not all people stood at ʿArafah; rather, only some of them did.

In the statement of Jubayr [ibn Muṭʿim], ‘It was nothing but a guidance from Allah for him,’ there is an indication that Allah had not revealed any specific revelation to him at that time commanding him to stand at ʿArafah. If that had been the case at that time, and Allah had commanded him to stand at ʿArafah in the presence of Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, it would have been more likely that he would have said, ‘So I knew that Allah had commanded him to do so.’ Rather, what Jubayr meant is that it was permissible for him to say that before the entire Qur’an was revealed—since the entire Qur’an was not revealed to the Prophet (ﷺ) in Makkah before his migration to Madīnah; rather, only some of the Qur’an was revealed in Makkah before the migration, and the rest after the migration to Madīnah.

I deduced that he meant by ‘before the Qur’an was revealed to him,’ the entire Qur’an, not that he meant before any part of the Qur’an was revealed.""

**CHAPTER:**"
"3059","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar who said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bakr who informed us Ibn Jurayj who informed me my father, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im who said:

""I lost a camel of mine on the Day of 'Arafah, so I went to 'Arafah searching for it. There I saw Muhammad (ﷺ) standing among the people on 'Arafah upon his camel in the evening of 'Arafah. That was after the revelation had been sent down to him.""

Abu Bakr said: ""If indeed 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Jurayj had met Jubayr ibn Mut'im, then this report clarifies that the interpretation of the report of Nafi' ibn Jubayr from his father is before the revelation of the entire Qur'an.""

**CHAPTER:**"
"3060","Narrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, who said: ""I went out to look for my camel at 'Arafah, when I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) standing at 'Arafah with the people. I said: 'By Allah, this is indeed the most intense heat! What is he doing here?' The Prophet (ﷺ) would stand at 'Arafah during the year in which he was there.""

**CHAPTER:**"
"3061","###
 Al-Makhzumi narrated to us, saying: ""From Muhammad ibn Jubayr, from his father."" He (Al-Makhzumi) also said: ""So, what is the matter that he (the Prophet ﷺ) exited the Haram? For the Quraysh did not go beyond the Haram; they would say: 'We are the people of Allah, and we do not exit the Haram.' And he did not say: 'He used to stand at Arafah during the years he was there.' The report of Rabi"
"3062","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa: Narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, on the authority of Ibn Rabi'ah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of a man from Quraysh, who said:

""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the pre-Islamic era standing at 'Arafat with the polytheists. Then I saw him in Islam standing in the same place. Thus, I knew that Allah had guided him to that [same stance].""

**CHAPTER:**"
